\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. A one-test-function Gaussian upper bound for the top stochastic Airy eigenvalue distribution and its scale family. The beta-two Fredholm identity and general joint laws remain unresolved.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the supplied complete definition, catalogue wording, scope qualifications and source list for this canonical ID. The original-source citations are inherited as supplied references; no fresh primary-source or novelty search was performed for this attempt. No current open-status claim is inferred from this calculation.

\paragraph{A direct variational probability bound.}Let \(f\) be a real smooth compactly supported function in \((0,\infty)\), normalized by \(\int f^2=1\). Set \(a_f=\int(f'^2+xf^2)\) and \(v_f=\int f^4>0\). For the stochastic Airy quadratic form in the statement, its value on this deterministic test function is a Gaussian random variable:
\[q_\beta[f]=a_f+(2/\sqrt\beta)Z_f,\qquad Z_f\sim N(0,v_f).\]
Indeed the stochastic integral of a deterministic integrand is Gaussian, with variance the integral of its square; the same value agrees with the pathwise integration-by-parts definition. Under the stated self-adjoint form realization the lowest eigenvalue \(\Lambda_0\) of \(A_\beta\) is at most \(q_\beta[f]\), and \(\lambda_1=-\Lambda_0\). Therefore
\[\mathbb P\{\lambda_1\le s\}\le
\Phi\left(\frac{(a_f+s)\sqrt\beta}{2\sqrt{v_f}}\right),\]
where \(\Phi\) is the standard Gaussian distribution function. The inclusion used here is \(\{\Lambda_0\ge-s\}\subseteq\{q_\beta[f]\ge-s\}\), which fixes the sign.
\paragraph{Scale test.}For a fixed smooth real \(h\) supported in \((0,1)\) with \(\int h^2=1\), take \(f_L(x)=L^{-1/2}h(x/L)\), \(L>0\). If \(A=\int h'^2\), \(B=\int yh(y)^2\,dy\), and \(C=\int h^4\), then \(a_{f_L}=A/L^2+BL\) and \(v_{f_L}=C/L\). Thus an explicit family of upper bounds results by minimizing
\[\Phi\left(\frac{\sqrt\beta}{2\sqrt C}(A L^{-3/2}+B L^{3/2}+sL^{1/2})\right)
\]
over \(L>0\). No independence between different choices of \(L\) is needed, since each is a separate upper bound.
\paragraph{Remaining obstruction.}The Rayleigh principle gives only one side and one trial state at a time. Recovering the full distribution requires controlling the infimum over all random admissible test functions. No Fredholm determinant or beta-two identity follows from replacing that random infimum by this deterministic one-parameter family. The calculation is an elementary variational bound, not a derivation of the source's requested identity or general eigenvalue laws.

